 read and and uh had a  big impact when i was a young man um in  a way one of the inspirations was what  happened to me you know after 9 11 as a  30 year old brown skinned you know  muslim named man living in a city like  new york and uh university educated with  a good income i didn't encounter that  much discrimination i suppose before  there was some  but it went to an entirely different  level almost overnight and i hadn't  changed but in a sense the way i was  being imagined by other people had  changed and that idea sort of lodged in  me that that you know it's possible to  sort of radically reimagine one's race  um or have it reimagined upon you so  what if it went the opposite direction  you know what if somebody like anders  woke up and had this happen to him and  how does without spoilers how does  anders handle that well andrew's first  doesn't want to believe it's happening  he wants to think that it hasn't  happened or that it won't make a big  deal or that he's the same person but  what he begins to discover is that even  though he is the same person he isn't  because um the way you're seen by others  does start shaping how you act and he  begins to act quite differently i mean  are we trying to get in this novel to  the point where race doesn't matter  anymore or you telling us something else  that it massively does matter and still  and still will do going forward well a  bit of both i mean i think um i think  race is something that we need to  imagine our way out of  i think it's an incredibly pernicious  idea that flattens people and puts them  in categories that are sort of made up  that said it's real  because we believe in it it does things  in our world  so what the novel does is it allows the  reader to go into a slightly  uncomfortable place where they have to  imagine what's going on they have to see  how that makes them feel they have to  sit with it and and then they have to  deal with their responses it's it's i  guess an invitation to go into your own  imagination and see how things shape you  i mean what are your reflections on the  way that society is right now you talked  about the way that you felt you were  treated post-9 11. what about right now  in 2022 how does it feel being in the  world as you well i think that for a  long time i imagined that things were  going to get better that in fact we  lived in a world where racism would  reduce for inequality would reduce where  the environment would get better et  cetera um i'm 51 and so for the first  you know 30 35 40 years maybe it was  possible to believe that but  increasingly it's become hard to have  that kind of optimism it seems that in  fact maybe we were at sort of peak good  environment or peak progressive ethos  some time ago when around 2008 2008.  barack obama the arc of history bends to  the good that was his face i think i  think that's right i think when obama  was elected there was a feeling that  things were going in a particular  direction  um and subsequent uh events have  suggested that that's not the case the  rise of strong men leaders all over the  place the continued environmental  degradation and and the rise of you know  overtly racist discourse in many places  and so i think that it's it's very  dangerous to be pessimistic about this  stuff i think we have to kind of find  our way to an optimistic viewpoint um  and and that's going to be a kind of  critical optimism so that's what we're  trying to do okay so expand on that we  don't have much time but how do you get  to that optimistic point because what  you're saying is like you've taken the  words right out of my head so a very  brief thing if you aren't optimistic you  wind up being nostalgic if you're  nostalgic your politics becomes let's go  back to how things were how things were  was rarely very good  to escape that you have to say you know  how might things be how might we want  them to be you know how do we get there  and the novel tries to do a little bit  of that it tries to say you know what  other way is there of living  how might that be what is the in a sense  racial apocalypse where everybody  becomes brown um and maybe  that racial apocalypse isn't an  apocalypse at all maybe it's the  beginning of something you know better  some opening and awakening wait time but  i could talk to you all morning but we  don't have the time love to speak to you  the book is out now it's called the last  point man it's available in all good  books awesome online i'm sure love to  speak to you thanks for being thank you
 

